            "content": "# Monorepo dev loop for the examples (2026-07-27)\n\nDesign for a single, working repository-level entry point that builds the addon\nand runs every example Storybook.\n\nScope: repository tooling only. No change to the addon's runtime behaviour, its\npublished artifact, or its release process.\n\n## Problem\n\nThree defects, all verified on 2026-07-27 against this checkout.\n\n**1. A clean clone cannot run any example.** `dist/` is gitignored,\n`packages/storyproof` has no `prepare`/`postinstall`, and pnpm does not build\nworkspace links. With `dist/` moved aside, resolution from an example fails:\n\n```\nIMPORT FAILED: ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND\nCannot find module '.../node_modules/storyproof/dist/preset.js'\n```\n\nThe loop documented in root `CLAUDE.md` and in both example READMEs —\n`pnpm install` → `cd examples/react-vite-sb10.5` → `pnpm storybook` — therefore\nfails for every new contributor. It appears to work only on machines where a\nbuild has happened at some point.\n\nThis is also the announcement's gating asset: \"a quickstart that works in a\nclean repo\".\n\n**2. No repository-level entry point.** Root `package.json` defines only\n`format` and `format:check`.\n\n**3. No watch mode.** Nothing runs `tsdown --watch`; editing addon source\nrequires a manual rebuild.\n\n### The premise that expired\n\nRoot `CLAUDE.md` states: \"No Turborepo — two workspaces with no\ncross-dependencies don't need a task graph.\"\n\nThat was true when written and is false now. `examples/*` depend on\n`packages/storyproof` having been **built**, not merely linked. That is a\ncross-workspace task dependency — precisely what a task graph expresses. The\nexamples arrived and invalidated the rationale without anyone revisiting it.\n\n## Decision\n\nAdopt Turborepo (`turbo@^2.10.7`), following the house pattern already\nestablished in the llame monorepo, with a single repository-wide `dev` task\nthat runs the addon watcher plus every example concurrently.\n\nRejected alternatives:\n\n- **Plain pnpm scripts with `--parallel` filters.** Verified workable and\n  dependency-free, but requires per-example root scripts that rot as the\n  examples set grows. Vue and Svelte examples are anticipated.\n- **Consuming `src` directly in development, skipping the build.**\n  `preset.ts` runs in Node on Storybook's server, and more importantly this\n  would make the contributor loop exercise a different artifact shape than the\n  published one — the exact class of defect release plan Task 8 exists to\n  catch.\n\n## Design\n\n### `turbo.json`\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://turborepo.dev/schema.json\",\n  \"globalEnv\": [\"CI\", \"NODE_ENV\"],\n  \"globalDependencies\": [\".node-version\"],\n  \"ui\": \"tui\",\n  \"tasks\": {\n    \"build\": {\n      \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"dist/**\"]\n    },\n    \"lint\": {},\n    \"typecheck\": { \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"] },\n    \"test\": { \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"] },\n    \"test:visual\": { \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"] },\n    \"dev\": { \"cache\": false, \"persistent\": true, \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"] }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nTwo deliberate divergences from llame's `turbo.json`:\n\n- **`outputs: [\"dist/**\"]`** where llame has `outputs: []`. llame's packages are\n  JIT source; storyproof emits real build output, so there is something to\n  cache and restore.\n- **`dev` gains `dependsOn: [\"^build\"]`**, which llame does not need. This edge\n  is the fix: it is what makes a clean clone work.\n\nllame's `transit` node is deliberately **not** copied. It exists to cache-bust\n`typecheck`/`test` without forcing a dependency build first. Here the examples\ngenuinely consume storyproof's emitted `dist` and `.d.ts`, so `^build` is the\nhonest edge and `transit` would be cargo-culting.\n\nNote that turbo forbids depending on a `persistent` task. The examples' `dev`\ntherefore depends on `^build` (one-shot); the addon's watcher runs *alongside*\nas a second persistent task, not as a dependency.\n\n### Scripts\n\nRoot `package.json`:\n\n```json\n\"build\":        \"turbo run build\",\n\"dev\":          \"turbo run dev\",\n\"lint\":         \"turbo run lint\",\n\"typecheck\":    \"turbo run typecheck\",\n\"test\":         \"turbo run test\",\n\"prepare\":      \"pnpm --filter storyproof build\",\n\"format\":       \"prettier --write .\",\n\"format:check\": \"prettier --check .\"\n```\n\n`packages/storyproof` gains `\"dev\": \"tsdown --watch\"`.\n\nEach example's existing `storybook` script is **renamed** to `dev` so turbo can\ndiscover it; `build-storybook` is unchanged.\n\n`turbo run dev` with no filter runs the addon watcher and every example.\n`apps/website` has no `dev` task and is skipped. Adding a Vue or Svelte example\nlater requires no root script change — that is the point of the single entry\npoint.\n\n### Why `prepare` uses pnpm rather than turbo\n\n`prepare` exists so the direct path (`cd examples/react-vite-sb10.5 && pnpm\ndev`) also works, not only `pnpm dev` from the root.\n\nIt invokes `pnpm --filter storyproof build` rather than\n`turbo run build --filter=./packages/storyproof` because `pnpm install` is\nroutinely run *while a dev session is active* — adding a dependency in a second\nterminal. A turbo-invoking `prepare` would then put two turbo processes on the\nsame repository, contending over the daemon and cache. Plain pnpm has zero\ninteraction surface with a running `turbo run dev`.\n\nAccepted costs: one uncached ~2.3 s build per install, and a cold turbo cache\nso the first `pnpm dev` rebuilds once (also ~2.3 s). Both measured.\n\nConsequence to keep in mind: a broken build now fails `pnpm install`. This is\nthe same build CI already runs, so it should not fail independently — but it\ndoes move the failure earlier.\n\nIf a git-hook manager (lefthook, as in llame) is added later, `prepare` must\nchain both commands rather than being replaced.\n\n### Ports\n\nEach example gets an **explicit, distinct** port, allocated from 6006 upward:\n\n| Example                    | Port |\n| -------------------------- | ---- |\n| `react-vite-sb10.5`        | 6006 |\n| `nextjs-vite-sb10.5`       | 6007 |\n| _(next example added)_     | 6008 |\n\nStorybook does auto-select a free port — `getServerPort` calls\n`detectFreePort(port)`, which scans upward when the requested port is taken,\nand `--exact-port` exists to opt out of that. Explicit ports are chosen anyway\nfor two reasons:\n\n1. **Concurrent auto-selection races.** `getServerPort` resolves a port\n   *number*, and `build-dev.ts` binds the server later. That is check-then-bind:\n   two Storybooks starting simultaneously under `turbo run dev` can both be\n   handed 6006, and one loses with `EADDRINUSE`. This presents as \"sometimes one\n   example doesn't come up\".\n2. **Documentation.** A documentation-by-example repository wants each example\n   README to state a fixed URL.\n\nAll examples pass `--no-open`. With several examples, the default\nbrowser-opening behaviour would spawn one tab per example on every `pnpm dev`.\nThe turbo TUI shows each pane's URL, and the docs name\n`http://localhost:6006` as the starting point.\n\n**The port allocation is a rule, not an accident.** It belongs in root\n`AGENTS.md` alongside the existing bar for adding an example, or the fourth\nexample will silently collide with the third.\n\n### Running a single example\n\nFour concurrent Storybook + Vite servers is roughly 2 GB of memory and four\nfile watchers, which is noticeable on WSL2. The escape hatch must be documented\nverbatim, because the intuitive form is broken — pnpm consumes `--filter` as its\nown flag before turbo sees it:\n\n```bash\n# Broken: pnpm intercepts --filter\npnpm dev --filter=./examples/react-vite-sb10.5\n\n# Correct\npnpm turbo run dev --filter=./examples/react-vite-sb10.5\n```\n\n### Supporting changes\n\n- `.gitignore` gains `.turbo`.\n- Root `devDependencies` gains `turbo@^2.10.7`.\n\n## Documentation this invalidates\n\n- Root `CLAUDE.md` (and its `AGENTS.md`/`GEMINI.md` symlinks): the \"No\n  Turborepo\" sentence is now false and must be replaced with the actual\n  rationale — examples depend on built output, so the graph is real. The\n  Commands section gains the turbo entry points.\n- Both example READMEs: the \"Run it\" block currently documents a sequence that\n  fails on a clean clone.\n- Root `AGENTS.md`: the port-allocation rule for new examples.\n\n## Hazards\n\n**tsdown writes into a tracked source file.** `exports: true` regenerates\n`packages/storyproof/package.json`'s `exports` map on every build. That file is\nnot a declared turbo output, so turbo never restores it — which is correct, and\nsafe because `tsdown.config.ts` is a hashed input, so changing the entry list\nbusts the cache and regenerates.\n\nIf anyone later adds `package.json` to `outputs`, a cache hit would stop\nregenerating it, silently defeating the `git diff --exit-code` drift gate in\nCI's `package` job. Do not add it.\n\n**Watch mode does not cover the server side.** A change to `dist/manager.js`\nshould trigger Storybook's manager rebuild, but `preset.ts` runs on Storybook's\nserver and still requires a restart. This must be stated in the docs rather\nthan discovered as flakiness.\n\n**Watch mode and the publish gates.** The build is ~2.3 s wall, of which only\n~159 ms is the bundle; attw (~569 ms) and publint (~592 ms) dominate. Those are\npublish gates and tell a contributor nothing mid-edit. Whether tsdown already\nskips them under `--watch` is **unverified** — check before adding any\nconfiguration to suppress them, and only gate them behind an env var if they do\nin fact run.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe implementation is not done until these pass:\n\n1. **Clean-clone simulation.** From a fresh clone (or with `dist/`, `.turbo`,\n   and `node_modules` removed): `pnpm install` then `pnpm dev` brings up both\n   examples, and the Visual tests panel renders in each.\n2. **Direct path.** `cd examples/react-vite-sb10.5 && pnpm dev` works after\n   `pnpm install` alone, proving `prepare` did its job.\n3. **Concurrency.** Both examples come up together on 6006 and 6007, repeatedly,\n   with no `EADDRINUSE`.\n4. **No interference.** `pnpm install` in a second terminal while `pnpm dev` is\n   running completes without turbo daemon or cache errors.\n5. **Watch.** Editing `src/manager/PanelView.tsx` rebuilds and is visible in the\n   running Storybook without a manual build.\n6. **Existing gates unaffected.** `pnpm --filter storyproof test`, `typecheck`,\n   `lint`, and `pnpm format:check` still pass, and `git diff --exit-code` is\n   clean after a build.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **Migrating CI to turbo.** CI currently invokes `pnpm --filter storyproof …`\n  directly and is green. Adopting turbo there (and remote caching) is a separate\n  change with its own risk, and should not ride along with this one.\n- Adding the Vue or Svelte examples themselves. This design makes them cheap to\n  add; whether storyproof supports non-React renderers is an open question\n  tracked separately.\n",
