          "text": "Two separate things, and they pull in opposite directions — worth separating before answering.\n\n## The two value propositions\n\n**Regression detection** — \"did my UI change?\" — needs only determinism. One engine in a pinned container does this well, and it's what committed baselines in Git are good at.\n\n**Cross-browser fidelity** — \"does this look right in Safari?\" — needs real browsers on real platforms. Playwright cannot deliver this, and **containerizing makes it strictly worse**, because Playwright's WebKit on Linux is the engine built against a Linux font and graphics stack, not the Safari your users run.\n\nstoryproof should claim the first and explicitly disclaim the second. Adding engines multiplies baselines, review burden, and runtime 3×, in exchange for catching engine-specific layout bugs — real, but a minority of regressions. So: **Chromium-only for `0.1.0`, more engines opt-in later.** The key already leads with `browserName`, so nothing needs redesigning to allow it.\n\n## The part that actually bites, and it's new\n\n**Container mode makes the image itself part of the environment identity — and nothing currently records it.**\n\nToday `compatibleMetadata` compares `browser.version` and `playwrightVersion`. That catches an image upgrade *when Chromium changes*. It does **not** catch two images with the same Chromium and different font packages. Same version numbers, different pixels, silent false diff — the same failure class we started with, just relocated.\n\nAnd you can't fix that by adding fields. Result 4 is the proof: bare WSL2 and the bare CI runner had **identical** font metrics (1352.34 on both) and still produced different pixels. Whatever differed — hinting, antialiasing config — is invisible to every attribute you could enumerate.\n\n## What I'd propose instead: a render fingerprint\n\nCapture a small fixed probe page once per run and store **its hash** in baseline metadata. On comparison, if the stored probe ha