          "text": "## How it works, end to end\n\n**The capture pipeline.** You add `storyproof/preset` to a Storybook. At dev-server startup the preset validates every option fail-closed (bad config kills the server with a named error, not a mid-run surprise), resolves the *environment identity*, and wires a `VisualTestRunner` onto Storybook's server channel. When you hit \"Run\" in the panel, the runner enumerates stories via Storybook's story index (the ≥10.5 requirement — older versions never expose it, and we fail closed there too), starts a capture session, and for each story: opens a fresh browser context (1280×720@1x, `en-US`, UTC, reduced motion), navigates to the story iframe, waits for Storybook's terminal render event including `play` functions, waits for fonts plus two animation frames, then screenshots — content-clipped to visible elements (portals included) or full viewport for fullscreen stories.\n\n**The identity layer — this branch's core addition.** Every session first renders a fixed probe page and hashes it: the *render fingerprint*. Every baseline is stored under `<platform>-<engine>-1280x720@1x/` and its `baseline.json` (schema 2) records platform, browser + Playwright versions, viewport, comparator policy, and that fingerprint. Comparison checks all of it before comparing a single pixel: any mismatch produces a *named* incompatibility — \"captured on darwin, renders on linux\", \"different rendering environment (fingerprint mismatch)\" — never a false red diff. This was built on measurement, not theory: we proved two visually identical Linux machines render different bytes, and that nothing enumerable can detect it. The fingerprint is the only mechanism that can.\n\n**Container capture.** Opt-in. storyproof `docker run`s the Playwright image matching your installed version, starts an exact-version browser server inside, connects over a loopback-only WebSocket, and rewrites story URLs to the gateway's resolved IPv4 (three measured traps live in that one command: Vite's host check, the unreachable IPv6 alias, Firefox's `$HOME` refusal). Approval still runs on your machine through the same path guards — only pixels move. Result, measured across all three engines: every machine produces byte-identical output.\n\n**Approval.** The panel's Accept promotes the *exact displayed bytes*: run/story/environment/sha must all match a completed result, the story's import path is re-resolved, metadata is revalidated, every write is confined to `storyRoots` by the path guards and lands atomically with fsync. Git diff and PR review are the authorization layer — that's the product's whole trust model.\n\n## Is it production-ready?\n\n**The code, for its declared scope: close to yes — with high confidence, because almost nothing is asserted from reasoning alone.** The full workflow passes 11 acceptance scenarios in four mode/engine combinations, 137 unit tests cover the sharp edges, the CI tests the actual packed tarball rather than workspace links, and the failure philosophy is uniformly fail-closed-with-a-named-reason.\n\n**The product: no — and the gap is not code quality.** Bluntly, in order of what actually blocks users:\n\n1. **It cannot be installed.** `npm i storyproof` delivers the broken alpha, `latest` points at it, and the release pipeline (trusted publishing, the pack-once/SRI chain, Changesets, `0.1.0-next.0`) doesn't exist yet. This is the entire critical path; everything else is secondary.\n2. **Zero external consumers.** llame is broken on the alpha; the \"one clean external project completes install→capture→approve→rerun\" promotion gate is unmet. Production-ready is an empirical claim and no one outside this repo has run it.\n3. **Support claims are still targets.** Ubuntu-only OS claim; Windows and macOS *host* capture have per-platform keys but no acceptance run anywhere; macOS-host container capture is measured-by-analogy; WebKit×container never ran the full suite. All cheap to close, none closed.\n4. **The trust boundary deserves one more hard look before announcement.** Storybook dev listens on all interfaces, and the README says plainly that anyone reaching the manager channel can approve baselines into your repo. Documented ≠ comfortable; a LAN peer writing files into your working tree is the kind of thing HN will find in an hour.\n5. **Known scope limits, fine for a preview but worth owning out loud:** one viewport, no masking, no CI-runner mode (the biggest ask real teams will have — capture in CI, review in PR), Playwright upgrades force a full rebaseline, container startup needs network for the `npx` fetch, and the fingerprint is blind to what its probe doesn't exercise (canvas/WebGL/webfonts).\n6. **The examples are still fixtures**, and they're the announcement's gating asset — task #7.\n\n**My prioritized order:** release pipeline → deprecate the alpha + icon → realistic examples → llame bump as consumer #1 → the cheap verification closures (WebKit-container run, macOS check) → then announce. CI-runner mode is the first post-announcement feature I'd build, because it's the difference between \"neat local tool\" and \"adoptable by a team.\"\n\nThe review workflow is still running — if it surfaces correctness findings, the \"close to yes\" above gets revised by whatever it finds.",
